The graph shows a curve $y = f(x)$ with two lines $L_1$ and $L_2$.
[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (6.0,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,5.5) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue] plot[smooth,tension=0.6] coordinates {(1,0.8) (2,1.8) (3,3.2) (4,4.8)} node[pos=0.5, above left] {$y=f(x)$};
\draw[thick,dashed,red] (1,0.8) -- (4,4.8) node[below right] {$L_1$};
\draw[thick,teal] (1.4,0.8) -- (4.2,5.0) node[above] {$L_2$};
\fill (3,3.2) circle (2.5pt) node[below right] {$P(3,f(3))$};
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]
Which line's gradient represents the instantaneous rate of change of $f(x)$ at $x = 3$?

Find $f'(2)$ using the drawn tangent. The labelled points lie on the tangent.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[->] (0,0)--(4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,7) node[above] {$f$};
\draw[blue,thick,domain=0.7:3.3,samples=60] plot (\x,{0.5*(\x-2)^2-(\x-2)+4});
\draw[orange,thick] (1,5)--(3,3);
\fill (2,4) circle (2pt) node[right] {$P(2,4)$};
\node[left] at (1,5) {$(1,5)$};
\node[right] at (3,3) {$(3,3)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Find $f'(2)$ using the drawn tangent. The labelled points lie on the tangent.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[->] (0,0)--(4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,8) node[above] {$f$};
\draw[blue,thick,domain=0.7:3.3,samples=60] plot (\x,{0.5*(\x-2)^2+2*(\x-2)+4});
\draw[orange,thick] (1,2)--(3,6);
\fill (2,4) circle (2pt) node[right] {$P(2,4)$};
\node[left] at (1,2) {$(1,2)$};
\node[right] at (3,6) {$(3,6)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

